t down, maybe they should check other services. However, the initial kubectl command showed all pods are running. So maybe the issue is with the service\'s endpoints or network policies. \n\nAlternatively, using get_metrics with different queries, like checking for errors or latency, could help. For example, querying for error rates or request latencies in Prometheus. \n\nBut given the tools available, the get_dependency_graph is a good next step to see the overall health of the service interactions. If that shows a problem, then they can drill down into specific services. Otherwise, check metrics for other services.\n</think>\n\nget_dependency_graph  \n"last_n_minutes=5"  \nSince all pods are running but the user-service\'s `up` metric is absent, I need to investigate service dependencies and potential communication failures. The dependency graph will reveal if services are properly connected, identify broken links, or highlight latency issues between microservices. This will help pinpoint if a service is unreachable or causing cascading failures.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=662, prompt_tokens=2791, total_tokens=3453, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc03-2a51-7a41-9c11-cb211cf1cf52-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2791, 'output_tokens': 662, 'total_tokens': 3453}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='15d4cfd1-a6fa-41fa-a227-495568300d51')]]
